    {
      "@type": "PropertyValue",
      "name": "description",
      "value": "Design a numerical experiment to probe the rigidity of the solution space for small feedforward networks by scanning hidden layer width N_c against constraint sample count w. For each w, train networks of increasing width on w constraints and measure the minimum width N_c(w) that achieves zero violation (max absolute error ≤ 1e-3) on held-out validation points. The hypothesis tests whether N_c(w) remains small and bounded (rigid) or grows unbounded (fat). We will use a simple synthetic constraint problem (e.g., polynomial interpolation or trigonometric function fitting) to isolate the network's capacity behavior. The scan will be performed with multiple random initializations to ensure statistical robustness. We will report N_c(w) values, training/validation error trends, and conclude on rigidity based on whether N_c stays finite and bounded across w."
    },
    {
      "@type": "PropertyValue",
      "name": "skill",
      "value": "None (custom experiment)"
    },
    {
      "@type": "PropertyValue",
      "name": "expected_prediction",
      "value": "N_c(w) will show a power-law or logarithmic growth with w, indicating the solution space is \"fat\" (not rigid). If N_c remains small and bounded, we will conclude rigidity. The surprise score (1.0) suggests we expect a non-trivial trend, likely growth in N_c with w."
    },
